[31m-# Feature Gap and Perception Gap Look the Same in Your CRM. They Require Completely Different Fixes.[m
[32m+[m[32m# What's the difference between a feature gap and a perception gap?[m
 [m
[31m-Reps log "feature gap" after a loss because it's clean, it's defensible, and it points accountability toward product rather than process or messaging.[m
[32m+[m[32mA feature gap exists when a product genuinely lacks a capability a buyer needed; a perception gap exists when the capability was there but the buyer didn't know it, didn't trust it, or wasn't shown it clearly. CRM loss codes rarely distinguish between the two, because reps log both as the same generic complaint, which skews product roadmaps toward fixes that were never needed.[m
 [m
[31m-What that logging behavior produces at scale is a dataset that systematically undercounts perception gaps - not because those losses are rare, but because they don't resolve cleanly into a dropdown.[m
[32m+[m[32m## Why CRM loss codes blur the two together[m
 [m
[31m-## Why CI programs are structurally better at finding feature gaps[m
[32m+[m[32mReps log "feature gap" after a loss because it's the explanation that requires the least interpretation. The buyer asked for something the product didn't do, or a competitor demonstrated something more compelling, and the rep records exactly what was said. That log entry is clean, defensible, and points responsibility toward product rather than toward the sales process itself.[m
 [m
[31m-The way rep debriefs work, and the way CRM loss codes are structured, creates a consistent directional bias. Feature gaps are legible: the buyer asked for a capability the product didn't have, or a competitor had something that you didn't. That's a clean story. The rep can log it, the CI team can aggregate it, and the roadmap request is in the system by the next sprint planning cycle.[m
[32m+[m[32mPerception gaps rarely get logged as perception gaps, because buyers don't describe them that way. A buyer who never saw evidence that an integration could handle their scale won't say "I wasn't shown enough proof." They'll say "the integration wasn't there," even when it was. The rep hears the same words the buyer used and logs the loss as a capability gap. The CRM ends up recording an absence that was actually a confidence problem.[m
 [m
[31m-Perception gaps are not legible in the same way. A buyer who didn't believe your integration could handle their scale didn't frame it as a perception problem in the exit conversation. They said "integration gap" because that's the honest summary of their experience, even if the actual integration would have performed fine. The rep heard it the same way and logged accordingly. The CI team aggregates "integration gap" without any signal that the real gap was a credibility problem in how the capability was presented.[m
[32m+[m[32m## How to tell the difference in practice[m
 [m
[31m-The result: roadmaps accumulate feature requests built on a dataset that systematically undercounts the instances where no feature work was required - just a better explanation of what you already had.[m
[32m+[m[32mThe distinction turns on one question: did the buyer ever encounter clear, specific evidence that the capability existed and would work for their use case? If the answer is no because the capability genuinely doesn't exist, it's a feature gap. If the answer is no because the capability exists but was never demonstrated convincingly, buried in a slide, or contradicted by an uncertain answer in the room, it's a perception gap.[m
 [m
[31m-## Where the pattern is most visible[m
[32m+[m[32mIntegrations are where this shows up most often. Few buyers test an integration before signing a contract - they rely on what they were told or shown. When two vendors both support a given integration, the deal frequently goes to whichever vendor described it with more specificity and confidence. A loss logged as "missing integration" is often not a missing integration at all; it's a missing demonstration.[m
 [m
[31m-Integrations are where this gap shows up most reliably. Almost no buyer tests an integration during the pre-sale process. They go entirely on what the vendor shows or tells them. When two vendors both have a relevant integration, the deal often goes to whoever told a better story about it.[m
[32m+[m[32m## Why this distinction changes the fix[m
 [m
[31m-A deal that logs as "missing integration" in your CRM is rarely about an absolute absence. More often, one vendor presented their integration as a strength - specific, confident, tied to the buyer's actual workflow - and the other didn't. The buyer's perception filled in the rest. The CRM says "integration gap." The buyer interview says something different.[m
[32m+[m[32mEach of these losses trends toward the same conclusion inside a CRM: log the objection, route it to product, wait for the roadmap item. That pathway works when the capability really is missing. It fails, and quietly compounds, when the capability was already there - the fix ships, and nothing changes, because nothing needed to change on the product side.[m
 [m
[31m-This creates a compounding problem. Each loss logged as a feature gap without an independent interview to test the attribution adds another data point to a CI picture that is structurally biased toward feature explanations. The roadmap request lands. The feature ships. The next rep walks into the same deal with the same competitive situation and loses for the same reason, which still doesn't make it into the CRM accurately.[m
[32m+[m[32mThe two gaps require different responses. A feature gap is a roadmap decision. A perception gap is a messaging, sales enablement, or proof-point problem - the fix lives in how the capability gets demonstrated, not in what gets built. Applying a roadmap fix to a perception gap spends engineering time without addressing the actual reason the deal was lost, and the next rep runs into the same competitive loss for the same reason.[m
